  <text x="745" y="464" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" fill="#006060" font-family="Courier New">Over-pressure: EWI cuts off injection first (before PRV venting) → zero waste pathway</text>
  <text x="745" y="480" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" fill="#00ffcc" font-family="Courier New">BENEFIT vs Phase 19: never heating more water than needed → 50%+ reduction in boiler energy use</text>
  <text x="745" y="496" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" fill="#006060" font-family="Courier New">Rhombic crankshaft option (if more alternator output needed in Phase 21): adds crankpin geometry flexibility</text>

  <text x="700" y="545" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" fill="#404060" font-family="Courier New">Phase 20 · Flash Boiler · EWI · Condenser Loop · 1 Gal Flash + 20 Gal Tank · 74 Sec Cold Start</text>
  </svg>
  </div>

  <div class="g2">
    <div class="card" style="border-left:3px solid var(--new)">
      <div class="badge" style="color:var(--new);border-color:var(--new)">Flash vs Drum — Why Flash Wins</div>
      <h3>1 Gallon Flash Boiler Advantages</h3>
      <div class="srow"><span>Volume to heat</span><span class="v isnew">3.8L only (1 gal)</span></div>
      <div class="srow"><span>Maintenance wattage</span><span class="v isnew">~1,000W (was 2,000W+)</span></div>
      <div class="srow"><span>Cold start (tested)</span><span class="v isnew">~74 seconds to pressure</span></div>
      <div class="srow"><span>Weight saving</span><span class="v isnew">~38 kg vs 48 kg</span></div>
      <div class="srow"><span>Pressure stability</span><span class="v pass">EWI keeps it constant</span></div>
      <div class="srow"><span>Safety</span><span class="v pass">Less water = less energy stored</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card" style="border-left:3px solid var(--water)">
      <div class="badge" style="color:var(--water);border-color:var(--water)">Condenser Loop</div>
      <h3>Closed Loop Water Recovery</h3>
      <p>Exhaust steam from both pistons is ducted to an air-cooled condenser mounted in the front grille position. Steam cools to liquid water and returns to the 20 gallon tank via gravity/pump.</p>
      <p>At 70%+ recovery: water consumption drops from ~440 L/min gross to ~130 L/min net. At 200 RPM for 1 hour, net draw is ~7,800 litres — but this is steam volume at 90 PSI, not liquid volume. Actual liquid water use is a fraction. Theoretical range exceeds 8,000 miles on a full 20 gallon tank with condenser running.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
